## Summary

Create an abstract transport layer that the WebSocket implementation can use for network I/O. This allows supporting both raw TCP (ws://) and TLS (wss://) connections.

## Rationale

WebSocket needs to work with both:
- ws:// - Plain TCP connection
- wss:// - TLS-encrypted TCP connection

By abstracting the transport, we can:
1. Use the same WebSocket logic for both schemes
2. Support different TLS backends (Zig std, OpenSSL, etc.)
3. Enable testing with mock transports
4. Potentially support other transports in the future

