## Documentation Content

### Create `docs/patterns/opaque-handles.md`

```markdown
# Opaque Handle Pattern

## Problem

Circular imports between modules that reference each other's types.

## Solution

Use `opaque {}` types as handles that can be passed between modules
without creating import cycles.

## Example: DOM Handles

```zig
// src/dom/handles.zig
pub const NodeHandle = opaque {}\;
pub const ElementHandle = opaque {}\;

// src/dom/node.zig
const handles = @import\("handles.zig"\)\;

pub const Node = struct {
    parent: ?*handles.NodeHandle,  // Breaks cycle
    // ...
}\;
```

## When to Use

- Breaking circular imports between tightly coupled modules
- Creating type-safe boundaries between subsystems
- Hiding implementation details across module boundaries

## When NOT to Use

- When types can be imported directly without cycles
- For FFI boundaries \(use proper FFI types instead\)
- When runtime type information is needed

## Debug Assertions

Always add validation in debug builds:

```zig
pub const Handle = opaque {
    pub fn validate\(self: *const Handle\) void {
        if \(builtin.mode == .Debug\) {
            std.debug.assert\(@intFromPtr\(self\) \!= 0\)\;
        }
    }
}\;
```
```

